Z||000000||fieroc4||01-24-2000||02:43 AM||||Hello all, I just joined and thought I'd ask about suspension mods.<P>I know people have modified the suspension on their cars, poly bushings, coil overs, +1,+2,+3 wheels & tires and sway bars, but has anybody taken their street car to the skid pad?  As I recall the Fiero pulled about 0.87g (please correct me if I'm wrong).<P>Engine mods are easy to quantify go to the "Time and Tune" at the local strip and see what the numbers say, suspension mods are harder to figure out.<P>Dan||||reg||6
Z||000001||Phaeton||01-24-2000||03:37 AM||||If there is an autocross club in your area join!  You will get to drive as fast as you can on a course set up between traffic cones.  Faster and faster until sliding off the track.  This eliminates a whole lot of guesswork in dialling in the handling.  I got my '81 X1/9 to 1.03 g's.  Still working with my Fiero, knocking 75# off the back (aluminum blk and heads) and dropping the front an inch.  Combined with other mods hopefully this will take the Fiero over the 1 g mark.  Will post if it does.  Meanwhile there are several suspension threads with an amazing amount of info on whats out there and what fits.  A search button is hidden that can bring up past topics by keyword.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||01-24-2000||08:46 AM||||I've got Koni struts eibach springs and many bushings have been changed. At Road Atlanta on a 200' skid pad (NOT me driving) a professional at the Panoz/Subaru driving school can make it pull 1.13g in both directions (right and left) If you can hook up with a SCCA driver/club and learn how to drive the car you'll be able to pull the same numbers! I'm not a very smooth driver i end up spilling the car more than anything... <BR>(on stock wheels running Yokohama A032)||||reg||1
